  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of minimal residual disease (MRD) and how dormant or residual cancer cells may circulate and seed relapse. A blood vessel runs through the center, with arrows showing exchange between tumor deposits and the bloodstream. On the left, a micrometastasis and single disseminated tumor cells (DTCs) are depicted as possible reservoirs of MRD, alongside a “cancer hibernation” state. These residual cells appear able to shed material into blood, including circulating tumor cells (CTCs) and circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA). Some CTCs are shown dying, suggesting that both intact cells and cell fragments or DNA contribute to what is detected in body fluids.\n\nA key message is that viable, metastasis-competent CTCs can persist in circulation and then travel to distant organs. Arrows point from the bloodstream to bone, liver, and lung, where new micrometastases may form, ultimately leading to relapse. The diagram therefore links dormant single cells, small metastatic foci, blood-based biomarkers, and later metastatic outgrowth.\n\nThe right side highlights open biological questions in MRD, especially how ctDNA accumulates, when malignant cells are released, and the shedding rates of CTCs and ctDNA.",
